Finding: {"title":"Duplicate stored-card insert bypasses the DB unique constraint's intended replace semantics — old token is never revoked","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Data integrity / orphaned resources","files":["src/server/routes/routes-micamp.ts","src/lib/schema/index.ts"],"detail":"`studentPaymentMethods` has a unique index on `(studentId, flightSchoolId, processor)` (schema/index.ts L7017-7019), meaning a student can only have one micamp card row. But POST /micamp/cards (L323-337) does a plain `.insert(...)` with no `onConflictDoUpdate` — if a student adds a second card, this insert will throw a unique-constraint violation from Postgres, which is caught generically at L349-353 and surfaced as an opaque 502 'Payment tokenization error' rather than a clear 'you already have a card on file, delete it first' message. Worse, if a caller relies on 'insert new card = replace old', the old MiCamp token is silently orphaned (never deleted via `deleteToken`), leaking a live tokenized card at the processor that the local system has lost track of.","impact":"Confusing 502 error on legitimate card-replacement attempts; orphaned card tokens left live at MiCamp with no local record to ever clean them up (delete-token happens only through the explicit DELETE /micamp/cards/:id route, which requires knowing the local row id).","recommendation":"Use `onConflictDoUpdate` on `(studentId, flightSchoolId, processor)` in the cards insert, and inside that transaction call `deleteToken()` on the previous token to avoid orphaning it at MiCamp — mirroring the pattern already used in `/micamp/connect` (L152-171).","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

